# broiler-js-conformance test262 floor 1
# The whole-suite --test262 ratchet. A later run of THIS revision under THIS
# manifest may not cross a row below. `atLeast` may not fall; `atMost` may not
# rise. Exhaustions are deliberately not held: a spent allowance says what the
# machine was doing, not what the engine does. A revision or manifest change
# re-bases with --admit and retires the old rows here with their reason.
#
# THIS IS THE WIDE MANIFEST'S FLOOR IN THE BASELINE NATIVE FORM, AND IT IS A
# SECOND FILE RATHER THAN A SECOND SET OF ROWS IN test262-wide.floor BECAUSE A
# FLOOR NAMES ONE FORM. It was set on 2026-09-15 with --admit from one merged
# whole-suite run of the conformance composition in the native form
# (JSD-0025), under broiler.javascript.wide with the suite's harness loaded, on
# one win-x64 workstation, from a build of commit 444f9c9, at the SAME
# allowances the bytecode floor beside it was set under: fuel 100000000 and a
# wall clock of 5000 ms per variant. That run's report is retained, compressed,
# in bundle jsb-11-001 (src/Broiler.VM.Profile.JavaScript/docs/evidence/
# jsb-11-001), together with the per-variant comparison against the bytecode
# run it was held to; the bundle, not this header, is where what the run showed
# and what it did not show is written down.
#
# THE FORM LINE NAMES A CALLING CONVENTION, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. It reads
# `x86-64-win64`, so a run in the System V convention (`x86-64-sysv`, which is
# what a Linux runner emits) is a different form to this file: without --admit
# the ratchet refuses and says the output form moved, and with --admit it
# re-bases onto that run rather than comparing against these rows. No System V
# run has been admitted here or taken on the machine this floor came from.
#
# WHAT READS THIS FILE: NOTHING AUTOMATIC. No workflow passes it to --floor on
# the day it was set; the lane's floors are the ones broiler-vm-lane.yml names,
# and the test262 profile workflow measures but compares nothing and holds no
# floor itself. It is held by hand, by running the conformance composition with
# --floor against a merged native report, and a run that nobody holds to it
# moves nothing.
#
# WHAT THIS FLOOR IS NOT. It is not acceptance of the native form and not a
# support claim for any runtime identifier; nothing in it has been reviewed.
# It says nothing about speed. Its rows are totals, not per-variant verdicts,
# so a run that holds them can still disagree with the bytecode form variant by
# variant; eng/compare-test262-forms.py is what reads that, not this file. And
# exhaustions are not held here, for the reason stated at the top.
suite test262
